(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-11-03"] = {"3.1":{"s":"This chapter studies motion in more detail. It starts with motion along a straight line, using displacement, velocity and acceleration and their graphs. Then it extends these ideas to motion in a plane using vectors, covering relative velocity and projectiles. It ends with uniform circular motion, where the speed is constant but the direction keeps changing."},"3.2":{"s":"In straight-line motion, average velocity is displacement divided by time and can be positive or negative; average speed is path length divided by time and is never negative. Instantaneous velocity is the slope of the position-time graph. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, the slope of the velocity-time graph. The area under a velocity-time graph is the displacement. For uniform acceleration three equations of motion link u, v, a, s and t.","f":[{"x":"v_av = (x_2 - x_1)/(t_2 - t_1)","n":"Average velocity: change in position over change in time.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.31"},{"x":"v = u + a*t","n":"First equation of motion (uniform acceleration): u = initial velocity, v = velocity after time t.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.34"},{"x":"s = u*t + (1/2)*a*t^2","n":"Second equation of motion: displacement s.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.34"},{"x":"v^2 - u^2 = 2*a*s","n":"Third equation of motion: no time in it.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.2, p.34"}],"w":["Average speed can be larger than the size of the average velocity: they are equal only if there is no turning back.","The equations of motion hold only for constant acceleration; use a negative a for retardation."]},"3.3":{"s":"Motion in a plane is handled by splitting it into two independent straight-line motions along x and y. Velocity and acceleration are vectors with components. The relative velocity of A with respect to B is the velocity of A minus the velocity of B. A projectile has constant horizontal velocity and a vertical acceleration of g downward, so its path is a parabola. Its range is greatest for a launch angle of 45°.","f":[{"x":"v = u + a*t (vectors)","n":"Motion in a plane with constant acceleration: use it as v_x = u_x + a_x*t and v_y = u_y + a_y*t.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.38"},{"x":"v_AB = v_A - v_B","n":"Velocity of A relative to B; v_BA = -v_AB.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.36"},{"x":"T = 2*u*sin(theta)/g","n":"Time of flight of a projectile launched with speed u at angle theta above the horizontal.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.40"},{"x":"R = u^2*sin(2*theta)/g","n":"Horizontal range on level ground; greatest, u^2/g, at theta = 45 degrees.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.40"},{"x":"H = u^2*sin^2(theta)/(2*g)","n":"Maximum height of the projectile.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.3, p.41"}],"w":["Resolve first: u cos θ is the horizontal part (constant) and u sin θ the vertical part (changed by g).","The range formulas assume the landing level equals the launch level and no air resistance.","At the top of the path the vertical velocity is zero, but the horizontal velocity is not."]},"3.4":{"s":"In uniform circular motion the speed is constant but the velocity changes direction all the time, so there is always an acceleration. It points towards the centre (centripetal) and has constant size. The angular speed ω is the angle swept per second and is linked to the speed by v = ωr. The centripetal force that provides this acceleration comes from real forces such as tension, friction or gravity.","f":[{"x":"omega = 2*pi/T = v/r","n":"Angular speed of uniform circular motion: T = period, r = radius, v = linear speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.42"},{"x":"a = v^2/r = omega^2*r","n":"Centripetal acceleration, directed towards the centre.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.43"},{"x":"F = m*v^2/r = m*omega^2*r","n":"Centripetal force on a mass m.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.43"},{"x":"T = 2*pi*sqrt(l*cos(theta)/g)","n":"Conical pendulum: l = string length, theta = angle of the string with the vertical.","r":"Balbharati Physics XI, 3.4, p.43"}],"w":["Centripetal force is not an extra force: it is the resultant of the real forces towards the centre.","Uniform circular motion is accelerated motion: the speed is constant, the velocity is not."]}};
